From c370569f69f0274db902078a58dac13418d62021 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 21:11:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(web#12): latch the last-good rig views, make the
 misses countable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An inline-3D scene tile blinks dark under GPU load. The weave pipeline was
ruled out live (zero missed submits, frozen id-match counters, blink visible in
the browser's own taskbar thumbnail), which puts it in the canvas CONTENT — and
the content is dark for one frame because the session told the page it was mono
for one frame.

The producer, DisplayXRWeaveClient::LocateViewsForRect, answers with NOTHING on
five exits. One of them is load-sensitive by design: xrLocateViews succeeds but
comes back without POSITION/ORIENTATION_VALID because the head tracker had no
fresh sample for this instant. Refusing to build a rig from an untracked pose is
correct — inventing eye positions at the origin would be worse. What was wrong
is what the renderer did with the refusal.

XRSession::OnInline3DViews overwrote inline_3d_views_ unconditionally, empty
vector included, and since patch 0032 UpdateInlineView consumes that on EVERY
animation frame with a `size() >= 2` gate. So one missed locate = one mono
frame: getViewerPose() hands the page a single view, the SDK renders one eye's
worth of content into a side-by-side canvas, and the tile visibly blinks. Under
load the misses cluster, which is exactly when it was reported.

An empty reply now latches. The previous views are held for up to 30 consecutive
empties (~0.5 s at 60 Hz) before the session concedes to mono; any non-empty
reply resets the run. The cost when the rig really is gone is half a second of
slightly stale head parallax, which nobody can see; the cost of not latching was
a dark frame per miss, which everybody could. Changes that genuinely end the rig
do not wait this out — session end and the last scene layer closing call
ClearInline3DViewLatch and drop to mono at once.

Two things fall out of the same read:

The mono view no longer churns. UpdateInlineView minted a fresh XRViewData on
every 2->1 flip, discarding per-view state the page owns (requested viewport
scale, the depth manager, visibility-mask event bookkeeping) and changing the
object identity behind getViewerPose().views[0]. It now reuses one mono_view_,
rebuilt only when the output size it was constructed with no longer matches.
(The stereo pair still rebuilds on a 1->2 flip; with the latch in place those
flips are rare, so that stays as it is.)

And the scene role survives layer churn. The session holds exactly one
inline_3d_scene_layer_ pointer, so a lazy-scrolled gallery recycling whichever
tile happened to own it nulled the pointer — which empties the locate rect,
which makes the producer answer empty, which drops every scene on the page to
mono. UnregisterDisplayLayer now adopts any other still-registered layer with a
scene scale, and only concedes when there is genuinely none left.

The producer got the observability the latch takes away. Hiding individual
misses is the point, but a silent producer would make a starving head tracker
undiagnosable, so every empty exit is now counted by reason and the RATE is what
speaks: a VLOG(1) dump every 300 misses (locate_fail / pose_invalid / no_rect /
no_session / time_fail), one LOG(WARNING) the first time pose_invalid passes 60
in a session, and the first hard xrLocateViews failure still gets its ERROR —
derived from the rolling counter now rather than a static bool that lied about
being the only one. The pose-invalid exit, the load-sensitive one, had no log of
any kind before. A rig that was never negotiated is not counted: that is the
inert negative control, already reported by the browser#92 weave-mode logging,
and counting it every frame would bury the real misses.

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-web#12
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
